# Data
*.csv
*.log
# ...except the performance evidence. Every number in the perf reports is traceable to one of these
# raw server runs, and `optimize/perf/README.md` cites them by name — so they are ARTIFACTS, not
# incidental output. (They were being silently dropped: the README listed a logs/ directory that had
# never actually been committed. Found and fixed in #62.)
!subprojects/Parametric-Indicators/optimize/perf/logs/*.log

# ...and except the harvested server archive (#94). This is a RECORD of work that existed only on the
# server: the 2026-07-13 end-of-day cap campaign and the 2026-07-14 precision investigation that built
# and verified the DEPLOYED champion set. The champions were committed; the code and logs that produced
# and checked them were not, so the deployed set was running while no longer being reproducible.
# Explicitly requested by the repo owner, logs and plots included. Nothing under here is live code.
!subprojects/Parametric-Indicators/server-audit/**

# ...and except the WS-EARN earnings evidence (#110). These are ARTIFACTS, not incidental output: the
# frozen Nasdaq-100 weight snapshot is what makes the company universe reproducible (index weights move
# daily, so a live fetch would silently change the universe between runs), and the timestamp table is
# the instrument every later stage of the earnings study depends on. Both are cited by name in
# optimize/earnings/SOURCES.md. Under the blanket `*.csv` rule they would have been committed as
# nothing at all — the deliverable would exist locally and vanish from the repo, which is precisely the
# "LOCAL is the source of truth" rule inverted.
!subprojects/Parametric-Indicators/optimize/earnings/data/*.csv

# ...and the TradingView economic calendar (WS-NEWS2, #114). This is the ONLY source found that carries
# actual + forecast + previous with a CORRECT UTC timestamp, back to 2013 — investing.com, ForexFactory,
# Trading Economics, FXStreet, Econoday and DailyFX all failed on access or on dates. It is 20 MB, and it
# is the raw evidence every consensus-based result in this workstream will rest on. Under the blanket
# *.csv rule it would exist locally and vanish from the repo — the "LOCAL is the source of truth" rule
# inverted, and the exact trap already documented for the earnings data below.
!subprojects/Parametric-Indicators/optimize/fundamentals/tradingview/*.csv

# ...but the blanket un-ignore above also re-admitted __pycache__ INSIDE the archive, and the data
# directory was never ignored at all. Both matter more than they look: they made `git status` on the
# server permanently non-empty, so the #94 preflight gate would refuse EVERY run as "dirty".
#
# A gate that cries wolf is a gate everyone learns to pass --allow-dirty to, which is strictly worse
# than no gate. Ignoring these is what keeps "dirty" meaning something.
subprojects/Parametric-Indicators/server-audit/**/__pycache__/
# NO trailing slash: on the server this path is a SYMLINK into the data tree, and a pattern ending
# in '/' matches directories only — so the slashed form silently failed to ignore the very case it
# was written for. Locally it is a real directory and matches either way, which is exactly why
# this had to be checked on the server rather than locally.
subprojects/Parametric-Indicators/data

# Output
*.html
*.pdf
dashboard_*.html
!subprojects/signals/stage1_0_reverse_signals/dashboard/*.html
!subprojects/Parametric-Indicators/frontend/index.html
!subprojects/Parametric-Indicators/frontend/l2.html
!subprojects/Parametric-Indicators/frontend/combined.html
# Hand-written report pages under docs/reports-*/ are DOCUMENTS, not output — they are the HTML
# siblings of the .md reports next to them and are authored, reviewed and committed the same way.
# Without this they were silently untracked: `git status` stayed clean while the file existed only on
# one machine, which is the exact failure #94 was about.
!docs/reports-*/*.html

